{"items":[{"owner":{"account_id":2516845,"reputation":2862,"user_id":9825,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/b01bd8a23b6e8bd0c02debddbea921ba?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"Zibbobz","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/9825/zibbobz"},"edited":false,"score":1,"creation_date":1634911561,"post_id":42062,"comment_id":89664,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"A tighter swaddle might help - we&#39;ll try to practice a little more, though it doesn&#39;t quite resolve the issue of the bottom flap not being able to wrap around our boy.  As for the other S&#39;s of soothing, they work to varying degrees - but it can still be an issue when we finally have to put him down."},{"owner":{"account_id":939235,"reputation":5323,"user_id":33055,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/zOso6.jpg?s=256","display_name":"Timur Shtatland","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/33055/timur-shtatland"},"reply_to_user":{"account_id":2516845,"reputation":2862,"user_id":9825,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/b01bd8a23b6e8bd0c02debddbea921ba?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"Zibbobz","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/9825/zibbobz"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1634911817,"post_id":42062,"comment_id":89666,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"@Zibbobz Any noise, other than white noise? Is the room dark enough? Sometimes even a night light can disturb the sleep."},{"owner":{"account_id":2516845,"reputation":2862,"user_id":9825,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/b01bd8a23b6e8bd0c02debddbea921ba?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"Zibbobz","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/9825/zibbobz"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1634912053,"post_id":42062,"comment_id":89667,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"Usually there&#39;s some ambient noise during the day - nothing we can prevent though. And also there&#39;s daylight.  We don&#39;t really have a &#39;dim&#39; room to put him in."},{"owner":{"account_id":939235,"reputation":5323,"user_id":33055,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/zOso6.jpg?s=256","display_name":"Timur Shtatland","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/33055/timur-shtatland"},"reply_to_user":{"account_id":2516845,"reputation":2862,"user_id":9825,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/b01bd8a23b6e8bd0c02debddbea921ba?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"Zibbobz","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/9825/zibbobz"},"edited":false,"score":1,"creation_date":1634912516,"post_id":42062,"comment_id":89668,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"Regarding ambient noise your solution with white noise sounds the best. Make sure that the white noise is loud. It is not supposed to be soft and low, but rather quite loud. Also, for light, have you considered a large upright standing folding screen? It can shield the child from light quite well."},{"owner":{"account_id":2516845,"reputation":2862,"user_id":9825,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/b01bd8a23b6e8bd0c02debddbea921ba?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"Zibbobz","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/9825/zibbobz"},"edited":false,"score":2,"creation_date":1634918687,"post_id":42062,"comment_id":89670,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"That might be in our future - for now though, the suggestion to tighten the swaddle has yielded some good results!  I may need to get more of the larger-sized ones since they seem to fit him best.  But barring any other surprises, I&#39;m probably going to accept this as the answer.  Thank you for all the help!"},{"owner":{"account_id":3674367,"reputation":72549,"user_id":9327,"user_type":"registered","accept_rate":50,"profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/jlp4Q.png?s=256","display_name":"anongoodnurse","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/9327/anongoodnurse"},"edited":false,"score":1,"creation_date":1635092272,"post_id":42071,"comment_id":89688,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"I was thinking of answering this, but you did a great job already! +1. I was REALLY bummed when I started menstruating three weeks after my C-section. I was so looking forward to lactational amenorrhea, but nooooo..........!"},{"owner":{"account_id":1665264,"reputation":2243,"user_id":7778,"user_type":"registered","accept_rate":33,"profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/f1edb320793125bf1d36ed27b87b9c51?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"localhost","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/7778/localhost"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1635099889,"post_id":42028,"comment_id":89695,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"@swbarnes2 my problem is I cannot be straightforward and say it to the other reason, sometimes I really wish"},{"owner":{"account_id":19170026,"reputation":81,"user_id":39221,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/91aa1adb0e8f50f17add990d43cd8ad6?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG&f=y&so-version=2","display_name":"buck","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/39221/buck"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1636295883,"post_id":41935,"comment_id":89803,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"Thank you for such a detailed answer! It is very helpful. I learned a lot from it. I will implement some of the methods to train my son."},{"owner":{"account_id":19170026,"reputation":81,"user_id":39221,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/91aa1adb0e8f50f17add990d43cd8ad6?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG&f=y&so-version=2","display_name":"buck","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/39221/buck"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1636296291,"post_id":41929,"comment_id":89804,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"Things have improved a little bit. Now he shows more interest in toys and the intention to move. He learned to crawl backwards."},{"owner":{"account_id":3674367,"reputation":72549,"user_id":9327,"user_type":"registered","accept_rate":50,"profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/jlp4Q.png?s=256","display_name":"anongoodnurse","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/9327/anongoodnurse"},"edited":false,"score":1,"creation_date":1636308634,"post_id":42122,"comment_id":89808,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"+1. As a person with a medical inclination, I usually recommended fragrance free detergents, and to double the final rinse for baby clothes if the child had any skin problems, even temporary ones."},{"owner":{"account_id":264052,"reputation":13329,"user_id":4087,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/X5OLy.png?s=256","display_name":"Becuzz","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/4087/becuzz"},"edited":false,"score":1,"creation_date":1636849345,"post_id":42149,"comment_id":89848,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"How would you recommend wearing him?  I&#39;ve never done such a thing outside of a carrier thing we can use to wear him on hikes.  I doubt that would be very comfortable for him.  And I&#39;m having trouble envisioning how to get him to fall asleep in there (he usually falls asleep as we rock him)."},{"owner":{"account_id":3674367,"reputation":72549,"user_id":9327,"user_type":"registered","accept_rate":50,"profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/jlp4Q.png?s=256","display_name":"anongoodnurse","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/9327/anongoodnurse"},"reply_to_user":{"account_id":264052,"reputation":13329,"user_id":4087,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/X5OLy.png?s=256","display_name":"Becuzz","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/4087/becuzz"},"edited":false,"score":2,"creation_date":1636855376,"post_id":42149,"comment_id":89849,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"@Becuzz - Google baby carriers. When mine were little, we only had these metal-framed monstrosities, and as uncomfortable as they looked, our kids fell asleep in them; today there are some truly excellent carriers. He will fall asleep; rocking is rhythmic movement with contact. So is carrying. Maybe a friend has one you can borrow? They can be extremely helpful."},{"owner":{"account_id":4948536,"reputation":19410,"user_id":11249,"user_type":"moderator","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/3RVO7.png?s=256","display_name":"Stephie","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/11249/stephie"},"reply_to_user":{"account_id":264052,"reputation":13329,"user_id":4087,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/X5OLy.png?s=256","display_name":"Becuzz","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/4087/becuzz"},"edited":false,"score":3,"creation_date":1636893898,"post_id":42149,"comment_id":89852,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"@Becuzz I am a huge fan of the cloth type ones, although they do have a bit of a steep learning curve. (Mine was a <a href=\"https://www.didymos.de/en/\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">Didymos</a>, basically just five yards of fabric, but a life saver.) There are also a lot of other soft carriers on the market and you can probably find a group of “fans” online or locally, where you can learn about tips and tricks and try them out. Having my then ca. 1yo baby on the back allowed me to explore the rocky shores on holiday trips with hubby and 4yo for example. And I wouldn’t have gotten <i>anything</i> done during year 1 of my first baby’s life."},{"owner":{"account_id":251740,"reputation":26679,"user_id":316,"user_type":"moderator","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/XDBbz.png?s=256","display_name":"Rory Alsop","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/316/rory-alsop"},"edited":false,"score":3,"creation_date":1636980511,"post_id":42149,"comment_id":89855,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"My wife swore by the cloth ones. I found them uncomfortable so went with a Baby Bjorn, which worked perfectly for me. We could get on with whatever we wanted - hiking, housework, gardening, and the baby slept like a log."},{"owner":{"account_id":132788,"reputation":524,"user_id":27439,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/9ca080b4ef1c6cb4df9bc123d3b3af61?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"psmears","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/27439/psmears"},"edited":false,"score":3,"creation_date":1637853635,"post_id":42160,"comment_id":89891,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"Also <a href=\"https://www.nhs.uk/live-well/eat-well/salt-nutrition/\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">limit the amount of salt</a>."},{"owner":{"account_id":2618090,"reputation":291,"user_id":13199,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/e82aedde5502d1f6ae10b9f5ea42a817?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"Chris H","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/13199/chris-h"},"edited":false,"score":1,"creation_date":1637858140,"post_id":42159,"comment_id":89892,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"<i>the first food you introduce to your child, you need to have something that can be swallowed without chewing of any kind</i> is true for weaning methods that rely on getting consumption going via puree.  There are other ways, widely accepted by health authorities, that allow them to learn chewing and explore food while getting their nutrition from milk, as discussed in the other answer."},{"owner":{"account_id":130645,"reputation":2112,"user_id":330,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/i6yM6.png?s=256","display_name":"corsiKa","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/330/corsika"},"reply_to_user":{"account_id":2618090,"reputation":291,"user_id":13199,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/e82aedde5502d1f6ae10b9f5ea42a817?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"Chris H","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/13199/chris-h"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1637908518,"post_id":42159,"comment_id":89897,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"@ChrisH Why would you possibly give an infant who has never chewed something they could choke on? And your claim that BLW is &quot;widely accepted by health authorities&quot; is dubious considering there is little to no research on it to say one way or another. But certainly, until you know baby can chew, you can&#39;t give them something that can get lodged in their airway. They can learn, and lead, on pieces large enough to actually learn but small enough that if they mess up they don&#39;t choke."},{"owner":{"account_id":5248597,"reputation":101,"user_id":37475,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://lh6.googleusercontent.com/-Z0N5iEvWN3o/AAAAAAAAAAI/AAAAAAAAASQ/xfNz19g-9RA/s256-rj/photo.jpg","display_name":"Stian","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/37475/stian"},"reply_to_user":{"account_id":130645,"reputation":2112,"user_id":330,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/i6yM6.png?s=256","display_name":"corsiKa","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/330/corsika"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1637916520,"post_id":42159,"comment_id":89903,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"@corsiKa You can buy smocks that are mesh-y. You stuff fruit in them and let the child play around with it. They&#39;ll soon be gnawing on it. Or you can give them food items that they cannot choke on, but still can &quot;eat&quot;. Approximately 100 billion children has learnt to eat, roughly 99 billion learnt it without modern precautions on airways and choking."},{"owner":{"account_id":2618090,"reputation":291,"user_id":13199,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/e82aedde5502d1f6ae10b9f5ea42a817?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"Chris H","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/13199/chris-h"},"reply_to_user":{"account_id":130645,"reputation":2112,"user_id":330,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/i6yM6.png?s=256","display_name":"corsiKa","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/330/corsika"},"edited":false,"score":2,"creation_date":1637918077,"post_id":42159,"comment_id":89904,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"@corsiKa I won&#39;t get far into that discussion here, but the UK <a href=\"https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/baby/weaning-and-feeding/babys-first-solid-foods/\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">National Health Service says</a> <i>&quot;finger foods and letting them feed themselves from the start&quot;...&quot;There&#39;s no more risk of choking when a baby feeds themselves than when they&#39;re fed with a spoon.&quot;</i> (scroll down to &quot;Baby-led weaning&quot;).  More detailed advice deals with things like grapes that should be cut up as they can actually lodge tightly in the airway."},{"owner":{"account_id":2618090,"reputation":291,"user_id":13199,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/e82aedde5502d1f6ae10b9f5ea42a817?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"Chris H","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/13199/chris-h"},"edited":false,"score":1,"creation_date":1637918145,"post_id":42159,"comment_id":89905,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"It&#39;s more the opposite approach - start on stuff that&#39;s too big to choke on - they don&#39;t have incisors to bite of little bits that can get stuck.  Learning on finely chopped stuff encourages them to gulp down without chewing, so isn&#39;t learning (it&#39;s valid, it just doesn&#39;t teach chewing)"},{"owner":{"account_id":2618090,"reputation":291,"user_id":13199,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/e82aedde5502d1f6ae10b9f5ea42a817?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"Chris H","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/13199/chris-h"},"edited":false,"score":3,"creation_date":1637918365,"post_id":42160,"comment_id":89906,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"Another set of guidance from a different national body (to demonstrate how widespread this advice is) is the <a href=\"https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/baby/weaning-and-feeding/foods-to-avoid-giving-babies-and-young-children/\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">UK NHS list of foods to avoid giving babies, with reasons</a>"},{"owner":{"account_id":251740,"reputation":26679,"user_id":316,"user_type":"moderator","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/XDBbz.png?s=256","display_name":"Rory Alsop","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/316/rory-alsop"},"reply_to_user":{"account_id":1877164,"reputation":141,"user_id":42117,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/5bd39afbb0ffd37a0ad4aaa51002370e?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"Failed Scientist","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/42117/failed-scientist"},"edited":false,"score":2,"creation_date":1637919711,"post_id":42160,"comment_id":89907,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"@FailedScientist - I&#39;m going to remove your comment, as it clearly goes against medical guidelines. You may disagree with medical guidelines, but on Parenting, we don&#39;t."},{"owner":{"account_id":251740,"reputation":26679,"user_id":316,"user_type":"moderator","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/XDBbz.png?s=256","display_name":"Rory Alsop","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/316/rory-alsop"},"edited":false,"score":1,"creation_date":1637919763,"post_id":42160,"comment_id":89908,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"Comments are not for extended discussion; this conversation has been <a href=\"https://chat.stackexchange.com/rooms/131802/discussion-on-answer-by-r-davies-any-alternatives-for-cerelac\">moved to chat</a>."},{"owner":{"account_id":130645,"reputation":2112,"user_id":330,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/i6yM6.png?s=256","display_name":"corsiKa","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/330/corsika"},"reply_to_user":{"account_id":2618090,"reputation":291,"user_id":13199,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/e82aedde5502d1f6ae10b9f5ea42a817?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"Chris H","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/13199/chris-h"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1637975759,"post_id":42159,"comment_id":89919,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"@ChrisH That&#39;s exactly what I&#39;m saying (and so is Bart), quartering the grapes for example. If you gave baby a whole grape, they could choke. If you quarter it, it might be uncomfortable, but they won&#39;t choke. i.e. they can swallow it without chewing. So, umm, thanks for making Bart&#39;s point in his post while simultaneously saying it&#39;s wrong. Neato."},{"owner":{"account_id":2618090,"reputation":291,"user_id":13199,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/e82aedde5502d1f6ae10b9f5ea42a817?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"Chris H","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/13199/chris-h"},"reply_to_user":{"account_id":130645,"reputation":2112,"user_id":330,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/i6yM6.png?s=256","display_name":"corsiKa","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/330/corsika"},"edited":false,"score":1,"creation_date":1638026126,"post_id":42159,"comment_id":89922,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"@corsiKa I agree on cutting up small round things.  But: Bart&#39;s answer says  &quot;you need to have something that can be swallowed without chewing of any kind.&quot;  I disagree.  You could give a baby a whole carrot, a whole banana, or a slice of apple, for example, and there would be no more risk of choking than with puree (says the NHS, who support both approaches).  It&#39; specifically small round things they caution against because they can lodge so much more securely,  and Bart didn&#39;t mention grapes - I did."},{"owner":{"account_id":22455149,"reputation":632,"user_id":41632,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/f907c700fb274a97da0acccb17df35b1?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG&f=y&so-version=2","display_name":"Libranova","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/41632/libranova"},"edited":false,"score":1,"creation_date":1638176183,"post_id":42149,"comment_id":89932,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"We had the same problem as the OP and we did exactly as anongoodnurse suggested.   In our case, they started to have trouble sleeping during the day in the early weeks. Driving around worked, but was not feasible for ~6+ hours a day, so I started to carry her with a baby sling. A lot of the bigger baby sling sellers also have how-tos on their webpage. E.g., the one from Didymos: <a href=\"https://www.didymos.de/en/babywearing-advisor/tutorials/tutorials-baby-wrap-sling/\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">didymos.de/en/babywearing-advisor/tutorials/&hellip;</a>). For us, our midwife showed us how too bind it."},{"owner":{"account_id":200040,"reputation":5117,"user_id":14336,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/7ce8091537385cbd2702461bd0f720b7?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"aparente001","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/14336/aparente001"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1639468327,"post_id":42180,"comment_id":89988,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"Thanks for the update.  So glad to hear about the progress."},{"owner":{"account_id":9197749,"reputation":2046,"user_id":26398,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/SzmpQ.jpg?s=256","display_name":"Pyrotechnical","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/26398/pyrotechnical"},"edited":false,"score":1,"creation_date":1639691595,"post_id":42207,"comment_id":89993,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"I&#39;m not sure you&#39;ve provide an answer applicable to the querent. They are inquiring on food options for a 4 month old, so I&#39;m unclear how information relating to an 18 month old is applicable. Can you expand this answer to bridge that gap?"},{"owner":{"account_id":2618090,"reputation":291,"user_id":13199,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/e82aedde5502d1f6ae10b9f5ea42a817?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"Chris H","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/13199/chris-h"},"edited":false,"score":1,"creation_date":1640094343,"post_id":42122,"comment_id":90031,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"There are also detergents that have very little scent; if you&#39;re on a budget these can be cheaper than the totally fragrance-free ones.  They&#39;re what I use anyway, and used for baby stuff.  You can also use less detergent than stated, especially if you&#39;re using a pre-wash for very dirty items."},{"owner":{"account_id":939235,"reputation":5323,"user_id":33055,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/zOso6.jpg?s=256","display_name":"Timur Shtatland","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/33055/timur-shtatland"},"reply_to_user":{"account_id":2618090,"reputation":291,"user_id":13199,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/e82aedde5502d1f6ae10b9f5ea42a817?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"Chris H","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/13199/chris-h"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1640095644,"post_id":42122,"comment_id":90032,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"@ChrisH I agree, the less scent the better for sensitive people. Although the vast majority of people who can afford detergent can also afford a slightly more expensive fragrance-free detergent. Sorry for preaching without knowing the financial circumstances, but in the long term health is more expensive than a good detergent. At least in the USA."},{"owner":{"account_id":2618090,"reputation":291,"user_id":13199,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/e82aedde5502d1f6ae10b9f5ea42a817?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"Chris H","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/13199/chris-h"},"edited":false,"score":1,"creation_date":1640096059,"post_id":42122,"comment_id":90033,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"@TimurShtatland I&#39;m always very sceptical of arguments like &quot;if you can afford to buy X you can afford a better version of X&quot; because they can be applied to almost all your expenditure and you&#39;d have to be super-rich to keep up.  All normal people have to prioritise, but those priorities may be forced, and certainly if there&#39;s actual evidence of a problem for a baby, that&#39;s top priority. Based on what I can buy, fragrance-free is 2.5&#215; more expensive per unit weight, and probably less effective (my normal choice has useful enzymes) meaning I&#39;d need more."},{"owner":{"account_id":939235,"reputation":5323,"user_id":33055,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/zOso6.jpg?s=256","display_name":"Timur Shtatland","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/33055/timur-shtatland"},"reply_to_user":{"account_id":2618090,"reputation":291,"user_id":13199,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/e82aedde5502d1f6ae10b9f5ea42a817?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"Chris H","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/13199/chris-h"},"edited":false,"score":2,"creation_date":1640097068,"post_id":42122,"comment_id":90034,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"@ChrisH I agree with you here: &quot;certainly if there&#39;s actual evidence of a problem for a baby, that&#39;s top priority&quot;. When it comes to living below poverty line, all my experience is not recent, and not very extreme. Perhaps we can ask for advice medical professionals who have experience with poor patients and their parents, or just ask other users with more experience of living while poor... I would treat my own advice in this matter with a <b>huge</b> grain of NaCl, I agree with you here as well. :)"},{"owner":{"account_id":3674367,"reputation":72549,"user_id":9327,"user_type":"registered","accept_rate":50,"profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/jlp4Q.png?s=256","display_name":"anongoodnurse","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/9327/anongoodnurse"},"reply_to_user":{"account_id":2618090,"reputation":291,"user_id":13199,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/e82aedde5502d1f6ae10b9f5ea42a817?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"Chris H","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/13199/chris-h"},"edited":false,"score":1,"creation_date":1640118914,"post_id":42122,"comment_id":90040,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"@ChrisH - Fragrance free is better for an infant/baby/toddler/child...into adulthood if odor isn&#39;t a problem. Fragrance is a VOC (volatile organic compound). If one lives below the poverty line, I would imagine the cheapest detergent that works and doubling the final rinse would be the next best thing. I grew up well below the poverty line, as did my parents. (Not complaining; I didn&#39;t know I was poor until high school!) It&#39;s a bit hard on one&#39;s health. Since rising above that line, I do go fragrance free for health reasons."},{"owner":{"account_id":3674367,"reputation":72549,"user_id":9327,"user_type":"registered","accept_rate":50,"profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/jlp4Q.png?s=256","display_name":"anongoodnurse","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/9327/anongoodnurse"},"reply_to_user":{"account_id":2618090,"reputation":291,"user_id":13199,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/e82aedde5502d1f6ae10b9f5ea42a817?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"Chris H","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/13199/chris-h"},"edited":false,"score":2,"creation_date":1640119527,"post_id":42122,"comment_id":90042,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"@ChrisH - &quot;if you can afford to buy X you can afford a better version of X&quot; just sounds like materialism to me, not common sense. Some things that are better for your health or that of the earth are more expensive initially, but profitable in the long run. (Plastics are cheap, but over the years have caused so many problems for humans using it as containers for food/beverages or clothing - gynecomastia in boys, UTIs in females, etc. Better (for those who can afford it) to use more natural materials, but there are risks to everything."},{"owner":{"account_id":2516845,"reputation":2862,"user_id":9825,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/b01bd8a23b6e8bd0c02debddbea921ba?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"Zibbobz","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/9825/zibbobz"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1640180101,"post_id":42232,"comment_id":90046,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"My concern here is that he <i>was</i> crying initially, then stopped after some time. Should we have tried to feed him while he was sleeping if that is the case?"},{"owner":{"account_id":4948536,"reputation":19410,"user_id":11249,"user_type":"moderator","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/3RVO7.png?s=256","display_name":"Stephie","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/11249/stephie"},"reply_to_user":{"account_id":2516845,"reputation":2862,"user_id":9825,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/b01bd8a23b6e8bd0c02debddbea921ba?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"Zibbobz","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/9825/zibbobz"},"edited":false,"score":1,"creation_date":1640180125,"post_id":42232,"comment_id":90047,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"In my opinion - no need."},{"owner":{"account_id":2001206,"reputation":2652,"user_id":21454,"user_type":"registered","accept_rate":20,"profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/98ec926f2315a761364265d0bb2f45e4?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"coteyr","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/21454/coteyr"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1640636419,"post_id":42232,"comment_id":90064,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"babies cry, it&#39;s one of their 4 primary functions. If he was in discomfort he would not have gone back to sleep. Think of the cry as an, &quot;Where am I? What&#39;s going on? Oh I&#39;m in a safe place in bed, fine I&#39;ll go back to sleep&quot;"},{"owner":{"account_id":200040,"reputation":5117,"user_id":14336,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/7ce8091537385cbd2702461bd0f720b7?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"aparente001","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/14336/aparente001"},"edited":false,"score":2,"creation_date":1640652639,"post_id":42247,"comment_id":90068,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"Downvoting because of &quot;cut back drastically on bottle feedings..&quot;"},{"owner":{"account_id":3674367,"reputation":72549,"user_id":9327,"user_type":"registered","accept_rate":50,"profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/jlp4Q.png?s=256","display_name":"anongoodnurse","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/9327/anongoodnurse"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1640886898,"post_id":42253,"comment_id":90082,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"What a great answer! Personal experience is a great teacher (although often very demanding. Sorry you had so much on your plate, so to speak.)"},{"owner":{"account_id":200040,"reputation":5117,"user_id":14336,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/7ce8091537385cbd2702461bd0f720b7?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"aparente001","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/14336/aparente001"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1640920329,"post_id":42253,"comment_id":90083,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"Great answer! / With weaning from the breast, we are told to reduce the nursing sessions one at a time, with a good number of days in between.  In other words, ramp down.  Maybe that would be a helpful model for reducing the number of bottles. / I wonder if eliminating sweets temporarily would help? / I bet that having a playmate over for &quot;parallel eating&quot; would help!  I doubt the playmate has to be exactly the same age.  Children are often fascinated by what other children do."},{"owner":{"account_id":3674367,"reputation":72549,"user_id":9327,"user_type":"registered","accept_rate":50,"profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/jlp4Q.png?s=256","display_name":"anongoodnurse","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/9327/anongoodnurse"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1641332082,"post_id":42277,"comment_id":90167,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"Yes, parents are the original bouncy (object of choice). Agree that the plane itself may do the trick. :)"},{"owner":{"account_id":14558581,"reputation":41,"user_id":42294,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/f200fbe797d630b5b86329b40bea86fc?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG&f=y&so-version=2","display_name":"Biscuity","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/42294/biscuity"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1641332767,"post_id":42277,"comment_id":90169,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"Knee bouncing or other manual bouncing doesn&#39;t cut it unfortunately. He knows the difference somehow. Agreed, there are limited options for most of the flight, but at times like when others are moving from seat to toilet, brief unrestricted moments could be used to try to get him to sleep."},{"owner":{"account_id":1780022,"reputation":63542,"user_id":6524,"user_type":"registered","accept_rate":80,"profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/40931bddde219f1a9e4514d2f4cfa826?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"Joe","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/6524/joe"},"reply_to_user":{"account_id":14558581,"reputation":41,"user_id":42294,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/f200fbe797d630b5b86329b40bea86fc?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG&f=y&so-version=2","display_name":"Biscuity","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/42294/biscuity"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1641333147,"post_id":42277,"comment_id":90170,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"@P_Jefferies More details of your seat might help here, but if you&#39;re in a regular economy seat and the infant is a lap child, there&#39;s certainly nothing you can do - there&#39;s not room. I&#39;d add more details to the question - is the infant in their own seat, or do you have a bassinet seat, or are you in business class or first class, that sort of thing.  Still, I think my default answer even in most of those cases would stand... it&#39;s hard to imagine anything working of the sort you describe, unfortunately, due to space and safety constraints.  The flight attendants will have some say there also."},{"owner":{"account_id":20553,"reputation":10800,"user_id":7798,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/431cd43aaf713caa80316e852a87a840?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"Paul Johnson","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/7798/paul-johnson"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1641392533,"post_id":42277,"comment_id":90172,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"Not an answer, but read the rules on luggage allowances and be creative. If you pay for an extra seat then a car seat underneath your child is likely to be free, which is handy if you are taking one anyway. You also get the full extra luggage allowance that comes with the extra seat."},{"owner":{"account_id":920526,"reputation":1885,"user_id":32290,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/vPzRc.png?s=256","display_name":"Flater","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/32290/flater"},"edited":false,"score":1,"creation_date":1641841039,"post_id":42247,"comment_id":90223,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"<b>Do not starve your child, not even partially</b>. This is irresponsible advice. Better advice would be to first offer solid food (the other tips are okay to try), and fall back on a bottle when it has failed (or to complete the meal if they didn&#39;t eat a lot of food)."},{"owner":{"account_id":2001206,"reputation":2652,"user_id":21454,"user_type":"registered","accept_rate":20,"profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/98ec926f2315a761364265d0bb2f45e4?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"coteyr","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/21454/coteyr"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1641870727,"post_id":42247,"comment_id":90230,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"I do feel I that I was clear &quot;Do this with the help of the doctor.&quot; being the first line, and &quot;Now, I&#39;m am not saying starve your child. I am saying let her get hungry.&quot; being in there too. Surely you can understand the difference between hungry and starving."},{"owner":{"account_id":2516845,"reputation":2862,"user_id":9825,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/b01bd8a23b6e8bd0c02debddbea921ba?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"Zibbobz","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/9825/zibbobz"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1642081124,"post_id":42301,"comment_id":90254,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"Yes, I do mean strapping onto his diaper more specifically - which then keeps the diaper wrapped around his body."},{"owner":{"account_id":1946899,"reputation":3943,"user_id":14484,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/d1606e78c0f8e9cf00a42043d5c3cf97?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG&f=y&so-version=2","display_name":"user1751825","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/14484/user1751825"},"reply_to_user":{"account_id":2516845,"reputation":2862,"user_id":9825,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/b01bd8a23b6e8bd0c02debddbea921ba?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"Zibbobz","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/9825/zibbobz"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1642115044,"post_id":42301,"comment_id":90255,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"@Zibbobz I&#39;d suggest you check carefully all around the edges of the diaper to ensure there&#39;s no rough, plastic type material rubbing uncomfortably against his skin. If a 4 month old is getting distressed by something like this, it seems most likely that something on the diaper is causing physical discomfort. If there&#39;s nothing obvious, then I&#39;d suggest you try different diaper brands, to see if this may help. They vary quite a lot, and we found that even some of the most common brands didn&#39;t work well at all."},{"owner":{"account_id":2516845,"reputation":2862,"user_id":9825,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/b01bd8a23b6e8bd0c02debddbea921ba?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"Zibbobz","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/9825/zibbobz"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1644935637,"post_id":42389,"comment_id":90495,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"He has been regularly making wet diapers (which is what the doctor told us to look for when we asked) and has only been fussy for a few weeks - and otherwise has been taking in formula if being a bit more fussy about it than in the past.  Thank you for this advice."},{"owner":{"account_id":22413077,"reputation":354,"user_id":42558,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://lh3.googleusercontent.com/a/AATXAJyreg9NdxdS77oR7IHA1khk8NLFhTyWdniK86an=k-s256","display_name":"Ryan","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/42558/ryan"},"edited":false,"score":1,"creation_date":1645058515,"post_id":42398,"comment_id":90523,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"Thanks for the answer. I think talking to him about feelings is very good advice and something we did better when he was younger and kind of got out of the habit of doing. It’s not an overnight solution, but I think it’s definitely important and likely to help."},{"owner":{"account_id":200040,"reputation":5117,"user_id":14336,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/7ce8091537385cbd2702461bd0f720b7?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"aparente001","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/14336/aparente001"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1645104407,"post_id":42398,"comment_id":90556,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"Great answer.  To supplement: Make sure older has some safe places to put items he doesn&#39;t want to share.  Teach older the trick of trading toys -- if he wants to grab Toy A from baby, it&#39;s easy if you just hold out Toy B.  Baby will drop Toy A when he reaches out for Toy B. Teach older gradually to verbalize his feels, e.g. &quot;I want some privacy now.&quot; &quot;I don&#39;t want baby to bother us when I have a friend over.&quot; (Some of this is looking ahead when the toddler is a bit older.) My older got so fed up with younger bursting into his room unexpectedly, we reinstalled lockable doorknobs for his room."},{"owner":{"account_id":22413077,"reputation":354,"user_id":42558,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://lh3.googleusercontent.com/a/AATXAJyreg9NdxdS77oR7IHA1khk8NLFhTyWdniK86an=k-s256","display_name":"Ryan","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/42558/ryan"},"edited":false,"score":2,"creation_date":1645111035,"post_id":42399,"comment_id":90566,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"@user21820 thank you for your input. I agree that consequences are important as well. We usually give him a warning the first time he does it and then put the toys away (not for a whole day though) if he does it again in the same setting which is rare. Maybe we can make a clear rule and then follow through with the consequence on the first offense. I also disagree with the spanking however. I don’t see any way of justifying hurting someone that can’t defend themselves. That’s what he’s doing to his brother, and if I did it to him, that would just send the message that it’s okay."},{"owner":{"account_id":22413077,"reputation":354,"user_id":42558,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://lh3.googleusercontent.com/a/AATXAJyreg9NdxdS77oR7IHA1khk8NLFhTyWdniK86an=k-s256","display_name":"Ryan","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/42558/ryan"},"edited":false,"score":1,"creation_date":1645111319,"post_id":42399,"comment_id":90568,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"… but I recognize I could be wrong about that. We’re all just out here trying to do our best. I don’t have anger issues, but spanking just isn’t for me."},{"owner":{"account_id":1086299,"reputation":257,"user_id":16319,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/B5cTn.png?s=256","display_name":"terdon","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/16319/terdon"},"edited":false,"score":1,"creation_date":1645111381,"post_id":42398,"comment_id":90569,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"Childless dude in his forties here, so please excuse my ignorance. I had the impression that this sort of abstract thinking, the ability to comprehend the concept that other people are as real as you are and have emotions just like you do comes relatively late. Is that wrong? Can a toddler really grasp the concept that people around them have emotions and can be emotionally affected by the toddler&#39;s actions?"},{"owner":{"account_id":22413077,"reputation":354,"user_id":42558,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://lh3.googleusercontent.com/a/AATXAJyreg9NdxdS77oR7IHA1khk8NLFhTyWdniK86an=k-s256","display_name":"Ryan","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/42558/ryan"},"reply_to_user":{"account_id":1086299,"reputation":257,"user_id":16319,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/B5cTn.png?s=256","display_name":"terdon","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/16319/terdon"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1645111622,"post_id":42398,"comment_id":90570,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"@terdon good question. I had to look <a href=\"https://www.parents.com/toddlers-preschoolers/development/behavioral/toddler-empathy/\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">this</a> up, but it looks like they can start learning empathy around 2, so this still seems like good advice"},{"owner":{"account_id":1780022,"reputation":63542,"user_id":6524,"user_type":"registered","accept_rate":80,"profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/40931bddde219f1a9e4514d2f4cfa826?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"Joe","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/6524/joe"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1645112424,"post_id":42398,"comment_id":90571,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"Good question for a separate question.  But as Ryan says, yes, <i>some</i> learning is possible at that point.  Much of parenting at this age is about slow progress - not expecting the child to instantly have the empathy of a 40 year old, but doing the work necessary to start the learning process."},{"owner":{"account_id":1131127,"reputation":422,"user_id":25837,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/016f3df2c1ded9b52755a1832612b762?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"user21820","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/25837/user21820"},"reply_to_user":{"account_id":22413077,"reputation":354,"user_id":42558,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://lh3.googleusercontent.com/a/AATXAJyreg9NdxdS77oR7IHA1khk8NLFhTyWdniK86an=k-s256","display_name":"Ryan","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/42558/ryan"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1645112567,"post_id":42399,"comment_id":90572,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"@Ryan: In case you missed the point, the palm-to-palm spanking is different from other spanking, because the goal is simply that both parent and child suffer a slight sting and nothing more. Furthermore, it has to be done cooperatively; you do not grab the child and force him to get a palm-spanking. Rather, as made very clear in my answer, it only applies &quot;<b>if you judge that he knows</b> his kicking hurts the brother&quot;, because only in that case would he be able to understand when you explain that such hurtful behaviour is <b>100% unacceptable</b> and he must get a palm-spank."},{"owner":{"account_id":1131127,"reputation":422,"user_id":25837,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/016f3df2c1ded9b52755a1832612b762?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"user21820","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/25837/user21820"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1645112754,"post_id":42399,"comment_id":90573,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"If he cannot even understand that his kicking hurts the brother, then spanking does not even apply, so all the objections against my advice based on the child being too young are empty. But some children can understand much earlier than others, and you would have to judge for yourself. Those who can understand will naturally know that your palm-spanking is mainly symbolic of an unacceptable behaviour, and the slight sting is shared by both, to implicitly affirm that it is not one-sided against him. Palm-spanking is not for those who have anger issues, but it works for those who do not."},{"owner":{"account_id":1131127,"reputation":422,"user_id":25837,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/016f3df2c1ded9b52755a1832612b762?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"user21820","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/25837/user21820"},"reply_to_user":{"account_id":22413077,"reputation":354,"user_id":42558,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://lh3.googleusercontent.com/a/AATXAJyreg9NdxdS77oR7IHA1khk8NLFhTyWdniK86an=k-s256","display_name":"Ryan","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/42558/ryan"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1645113183,"post_id":42399,"comment_id":90575,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"@Ryan: By the way, I assumed my advice about the toys was clear but apparently not. Obviously we cannot just silently wait for the child to violate the rule 3 times and then suddenly jump to the final consequence! As you seem to have realized in your comment, you should explicitly lay down the consequence on each repeated offence. The first time you don&#39;t even have to deal out consequences, but simply return the toy taken and warn that his toys would be taken away for an hour if he does it again. On the second time you follow through and warn that the third time it will be for a day."},{"owner":{"account_id":24290295,"reputation":1,"user_id":42571,"user_type":"unregistered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/219087ca1c63ee072118a6222305329b?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"sammy","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/42571/sammy"},"edited":false,"score":1,"creation_date":1645113335,"post_id":42399,"comment_id":90579,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"Can someone please tell me what “palm to palm spanking” is? I tried to Google it but only perverted stuff comes up…thanks in advance."},{"owner":{"account_id":1131127,"reputation":422,"user_id":25837,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/016f3df2c1ded9b52755a1832612b762?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"user21820","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/25837/user21820"},"reply_to_user":{"account_id":22413077,"reputation":354,"user_id":42558,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://lh3.googleusercontent.com/a/AATXAJyreg9NdxdS77oR7IHA1khk8NLFhTyWdniK86an=k-s256","display_name":"Ryan","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/42558/ryan"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1645113486,"post_id":42399,"comment_id":90576,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"@Ryan: One last thing; the fact that you are trying to do your best is enough; don&#39;t worry too much about whether you are giving too much or too little punishment, but try to make your parenting as consistent and fair as possible. Remember, your baby is just as much entitled to a safe environment as his elder brother, so being fair to your baby <b>requires</b> you to deal out barely sufficient punishment to <b>prevent</b> the older one from repeating the undeniably harmful behaviour (kicking) to the baby. If you can stop the behaviour without punishment, why not? But some children need a little bit."},{"owner":{"account_id":1131127,"reputation":422,"user_id":25837,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/016f3df2c1ded9b52755a1832612b762?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"user21820","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/25837/user21820"},"reply_to_user":{"account_id":24290295,"reputation":1,"user_id":42571,"user_type":"unregistered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/219087ca1c63ee072118a6222305329b?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"sammy","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/42571/sammy"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1645113672,"post_id":42399,"comment_id":90580,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"@sammy It is just plain English; you use your open palm to hit the open palm of the child. Not palm to buttocks, not slapping, not using a stick (or other objects)."},{"owner":{"account_id":1131127,"reputation":422,"user_id":25837,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/016f3df2c1ded9b52755a1832612b762?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"user21820","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/25837/user21820"},"reply_to_user":{"account_id":1086299,"reputation":257,"user_id":16319,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/B5cTn.png?s=256","display_name":"terdon","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/16319/terdon"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1645113863,"post_id":42398,"comment_id":90582,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"@terdon: It varies. Some children start early from 2, while others may not until as late as 5-6. The more the child is involved in taking care of younger siblings, the earlier they develop empathy, on average. Incidentally, having an older sibling care for them also helps children to develop empathy faster."},{"owner":{"account_id":4948536,"reputation":19410,"user_id":11249,"user_type":"moderator","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/3RVO7.png?s=256","display_name":"Stephie","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/11249/stephie"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1645114831,"post_id":42399,"comment_id":90584,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"The majority of the comments here were deleted because they were not for clarification. Note that per the <i>current</i> community consensus we will allow answers that suggest spanking and similar approaches (within certain limits). This does not mean that we necessarily condone this. We are very aware of the fact that many countries ban any form of physical violence against children. The SE system encourages voting on all posts, <a href=\"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/help/privileges/vote-up\">up</a> and <a href=\"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/help/privileges/vote-down\">down</a> as users deem appropriate."},{"owner":{"account_id":4948536,"reputation":19410,"user_id":11249,"user_type":"moderator","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/3RVO7.png?s=256","display_name":"Stephie","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/11249/stephie"},"edited":false,"score":3,"creation_date":1645120216,"post_id":42399,"comment_id":90587,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"@user21820 Unless you are a lawyer and well-versed in the topic, please refrain from rekindling the discussion."},{"owner":{"account_id":3674367,"reputation":72549,"user_id":9327,"user_type":"registered","accept_rate":50,"profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/jlp4Q.png?s=256","display_name":"anongoodnurse","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/9327/anongoodnurse"},"reply_to_user":{"account_id":1086299,"reputation":257,"user_id":16319,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/B5cTn.png?s=256","display_name":"terdon","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/16319/terdon"},"edited":false,"score":1,"creation_date":1645197483,"post_id":42398,"comment_id":90595,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"@terdon - On some level, this is present almost from birth. Experiments on <i>infants</i> show they can differentiate and show signs of empathy to a mother&#39;s feelings."},{"owner":{"account_id":1946899,"reputation":3943,"user_id":14484,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/d1606e78c0f8e9cf00a42043d5c3cf97?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG&f=y&so-version=2","display_name":"user1751825","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/14484/user1751825"},"reply_to_user":{"account_id":2516845,"reputation":2862,"user_id":9825,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/b01bd8a23b6e8bd0c02debddbea921ba?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"Zibbobz","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/9825/zibbobz"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1685356968,"post_id":42301,"comment_id":92964,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"Why the down-vote?"},{"owner":{"account_id":2516845,"reputation":2862,"user_id":9825,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/b01bd8a23b6e8bd0c02debddbea921ba?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"Zibbobz","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/9825/zibbobz"},"edited":false,"score":2,"creation_date":1686055471,"post_id":42301,"comment_id":92990,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"It&#39;s not my downvote, but I suspect it&#39;s because the answer here is phrased as more of a question to clarify the original question - you may want to edit it to be more clear that you&#39;re offering an answer (which you are) so that it&#39;s clear what your intention is."},{"owner":{"account_id":1946899,"reputation":3943,"user_id":14484,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/d1606e78c0f8e9cf00a42043d5c3cf97?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG&f=y&so-version=2","display_name":"user1751825","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/14484/user1751825"},"reply_to_user":{"account_id":2516845,"reputation":2862,"user_id":9825,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/b01bd8a23b6e8bd0c02debddbea921ba?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"Zibbobz","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/9825/zibbobz"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1686055719,"post_id":42301,"comment_id":92991,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"Thanks @Zibbobz That makes sense. I&#39;ve re-phrased it as a suggestion, rather than a question."}],"has_more":false,"quota_max":300,"quota_remaining":262}